      "id": "grammar2",
      "icon": "📖",
      "title": "Grammar",
      "subtitle": "Il-grammatika — the grammar of Lesson 2",
      "intro": "These are the grammar points covered in the slides. They build on the article from Lesson 1 and set up the agreement rules you'll see throughout the course.",
      "rules": [
        {
          "id": "noun-gender",
          "title": "Noun gender — masculine or feminine",
          "explanation": "Every Maltese noun is either masculine (maskil) or feminine (femminil). It matters because adjectives, verbs and demonstratives all have to AGREE with the noun's gender. Often (not always): masculine nouns end in a consonant or in -u / -i; feminine nouns end in -a.",
          "examples": [
            {"mt": "raġel", "en": "man (m)"},
            {"mt": "kelb", "en": "dog (m)"},
            {"mt": "nannu", "en": "grandfather (m, -u)"},
            {"mt": "ktieb", "en": "book (m)"},
            {"mt": "mara", "en": "woman (f, -a)"},
            {"mt": "kelba", "en": "female dog (f, -a)"},
            {"mt": "tazza", "en": "glass (f, -a)"},
            {"mt": "karozza", "en": "car (f, -a)"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "adjective-agreement",
          "title": "Adjective agreement — three forms",
          "explanation": "Most Maltese adjectives have THREE forms: masculine, feminine, and plural. You match the form to the noun. The pattern often follows: masculine ends in a consonant; feminine adds -a; plural takes a special collective form (often with vowel changes inside the word).",
          "examples": [
            {
              "mt": "raġel twil",
              "en": "a tall man (m)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "mara twila",
              "en": "a tall woman (f)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "tfal twal",
              "en": "tall children (pl)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "ktieb sabiħ",
              "en": "a beautiful book (m)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "fjura sabiħa",
              "en": "a beautiful flower (f)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "fjuri sbieħ",
              "en": "beautiful flowers (pl)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "colour-agreement",
          "title": "Colours follow the same agreement",
          "explanation": "Colours behave like adjectives — they have three forms and match the noun. A few colours (blu, ċelesti, roża, vjola, oranġjo, kannella) are borrowed and don't change form.",
          "letters": [
            "Abjad/Bajda/Bojod",
            "Iswed/Sewda/Suwed",
            "Aħmar/Ħamra/Ħomor",
            "Isfar/Safra/Sofor",
            "Aħdar/Ħadra/Ħodor"
          ],
          "examples": [
            {
              "mt": "flokk abjad",
              "en": "a white t-shirt (m)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "mejda bajda",
              "en": "a white table (f)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "fniek bojod",
              "en": "white rabbits (pl)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "vann aħmar",
              "en": "a red van (m)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "zalza ħamra",
              "en": "red sauce (f)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "għasafar ħomor",
              "en": "red birds (pl)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "ġakketta blu",
              "en": "a blue jacket — blu doesn't change"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "numbers-noun-rule",
          "title": "Numbers 2–10 use plural; 11+ uses singular",
          "explanation": "Important rule: after numbers from 2 to 10 the noun goes in the PLURAL form. After numbers from 11 to 99 the noun stays SINGULAR. Watch the difference between 'għaxar snin' (10 years — plural) and 'tlieta u għoxrin sena' (23 years — singular).",
          "examples": [
            {
              "mt": "Għandi tliet snin",
              "en": "I am 3 years old (3 + plural noun)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "Għandi għaxar snin",
              "en": "I am 10 years old (10 + plural noun)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "Għandi tlieta u għoxrin sena",
              "en": "I am 23 years old (23 + singular noun)"
            },
            {
              "mt": "Għandi ħamsa u ħamsin sena",
              "en": "I am 55 years old (55 + singular noun)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "ordinals",
          "title": "Ordinal numbers 1–10",
          "explanation": "Ordinals show order or rank. They're used with the definite article (L-ewwel, It-tieni, etc.). From 11 onwards, just use the cardinal number with the definite article (e.g., il-wieħed u għoxrin = 21st).",
          "letters": [
